🧭 1️⃣ cd
    Meaning: Change Directory — used to move into the folder (project) where your code is located.
    Example: cd C:\Users\Ashok\PycharmProjects\MyProject

🧾 2️⃣ git add ./filenames
            or
      git add .
    Meaning: Adds files from your working directory to the staging area, preparing them for commit.
    . → means all files, ./filenames → means specific files

🧩 3️⃣ git commit -m "message"
    Meaning: Saves (commits) the staged changes into the local (System/pc) Git history with a message describing the change.
    -m → stands for message
    Example: git commit -m "Added login feature"

🌐 4️⃣ git remote add origin <URL>
    Meaning: Links your local repository to a remote GitHub repository.
    remote → refers to the online repository (like GitHub).
    add → adds a connection to that remote.
    origin → a default name given to the remote repository (you can change it, but “origin” is standard).

🌳 5️⃣ git branch -M main
    Meaning: Renames the current branch to main.
    -M → stands for move/rename forcefully (it overwrites if “main” already exists).

🚀 6️⃣ git push -u origin main
    Meaning: Uploads (pushes) your local commits to the remote repository (origin) on the main branch.
    -u → sets the upstream connection, so in future you can just use "git push" without typing the full command again.
    origin → remote repository name.
    main → branch name you are pushing to.

7. To see all the branches created:
    Syntax: git branch -a

8. To create a new branch and move to it immediately.
    git checkout → used to switch branches, -b → stands for branch (tells Git to create a new one).
    Syntax: git checkout -b <new-branch-name>
    EX: git checkout -b feature-login

9. To create the same branch on GitHub and links it with the local branch for future pushes and pulls.
    Syntax: git push --set-upstream origin <new_feature_name>
    EX: git push --set-upstream origin feature-login
    git push → uploads your commits to GitHub
    --set-upstream/-u → links your local branch with the remote branch so next time you can just type git push or git pull
    origin → the name of your remote repository (GitHub)
    feature-login → your branch name

10. To Restore/remove from staging (if you accidentally added wrong file)
    Syntax: $ git restore --staged <file_name>
    EX: $ git restore --staged TestingConcepts/python_for_testers15.py

12. git fetch:
    Syntax: git fetch origin
    git fetch downloads the latest changes from all remote branches and updates your remote-tracking branches in your local repository.
    It does not modify your current working branch — you must merge or rebase to apply the changes.

13. git pull:
    Downloads commits from the remote repository and immediately merges them into your current branch.
    Equivalent to git fetch + git merge in one step
    Updates your working files immediately
    Could lead to merge conflicts if local changes differ from remote

