push your local code to GitHub for the first time:
==================================================
1. Go to your project folder
Syntax: cd path/to/your/project
EX: cd C:\Users\Ashok\PycharmProjects\MyProject

2. Initialize Git (if not already done) - This makes your local folder a Git repository.
Syntax: git init

3. Add your files to Git tracking
Syntax: git add .           (. means add all files)
                or
Syntax: git add filename1 filename2......   (folder name/filename means add specific files))

4. Commit your code
Syntax: git commit -m "Initial commit"      (You can write meaningful name inside "...text...")

5. Link your local repo to GitHub (remote repository)
Syntax: git remote add origin https://github.com/yourusername/your-repo-name.git

6. Push your code to GitHub.    (If your GitHub repo is new and empty, push the main branch)
Syntax: git branch -M main
        git push -u origin main

Your code is now uploaded to GitHub. Next time, just use:
=========================================================
git add .
git commit -m "your message"
git push

To Clone Project from GitHub to local
======================================
Clone (in Git):
Cloning means creating a local copy of a remote Git repository (like from GitHub) on your computer.
It downloads all the project files, history, and branches so you can work on them locally.

Steps for cloning:
1. Open Terminal / Command Prompt / Git Bash

2. Navigate to the folder where you want to keep the project
Syntax: cd path/to/your/folder
EX: cd C:\Users\Ashok\PycharmProjects

3. Clone the repository using its GitHub URL
Syntax: git clone https://github.com/username/repository-name.git

